---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/FurtherReading.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import ChartGrammar from '../../components/ChartGrammar.astro';
import { ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'The Big Three', href: '/explore/the-big-three/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Big Three: Sun, Moon, and Rising Sign"
  description="Your Sun, Moon, and rising sign explained in plain language: what each describes, how they differ, and why they make more sense when read together."
  path="/explore/the-big-three/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>The Big Three: three different jobs in one chart</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          The Sun, Moon, and rising sign are useful starting points, but not because they give you
          three personality labels. Each does a different job. The useful question is how those
          three functions work together in one person.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.ripplesSunset.src} srcset={ocean.ripplesSunset.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.ripplesSunset.alt} width={ocean.ripplesSunset.width} height={ocean.ripplesSunset.height} fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose flow">
        <h2>Start with the job, not the label</h2>
        <p>
          These are starting shortcuts, not finished interpretations. The Sun can help us ask about
          vitality, identity, purpose, and authorship. The Moon brings us toward instinct, habit,
          memory, care, and what feels sustaining. The rising sign begins at the eastern horizon and
          helps describe how a person enters experience while anchoring the chart's house structure.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="big-three-grid">
        <article class="big-three-card">
          <span class="big-three-card__glyph" aria-hidden="true">☉</span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Sun</p>
          <h2>What am I becoming or authoring?</h2>
          <p>Start with vitality, purpose, identity, visibility, and the impulse to become more fully yourself.</p>
          <a href="/explore/planets/sun/">Explore the Sun</a>
        </article>
        <article class="big-three-card">
          <span class="big-three-card__glyph" aria-hidden="true">☽</span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Moon</p>
          <h2>What feels familiar and sustaining?</h2>
          <p>Start with instinct, emotional needs, memory, habit, care, and the conditions that help you settle.</p>
          <a href="/explore/planets/moon/">Explore the Moon</a>
        </article>
        <article class="big-three-card">
          <span class="big-three-card__glyph" aria-hidden="true">↑</span>
          <p class="eyebrow">Rising sign</p>
          <h2>How do I enter experience?</h2>
          <p>Start with the eastern horizon, your way of meeting experience, and the point that anchors the houses.</p>
          <a href="/explore/angles/">Explore the Ascendant and angles</a>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="prose flow synthesis">
        <h2>Read them as a conversation</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Name each job separately.</strong> Do not ask one placement to explain the whole person.</li>
          <li><strong>Notice the styles.</strong> What signs are involved, and do they reinforce or complicate one another?</li>
          <li><strong>Notice the relationships.</strong> Are the Sun and Moon connected by aspect? What else is tied into them?</li>
          <li><strong>Add the rest of the chart.</strong> Houses, rulers, other planets, timing, and lived experience change the picture.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          If the three seem to want different things, I do not see that as a flaw to fix. Internal
          complexity is often part of what makes the chart useful to discuss.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ChartGrammar heading="The Big Three are a beginning. This is the larger grammar." />
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={ocean.lightRays} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The Sun: a central organizing principle</h2>
      <p>
        When someone asks "what's your sign," they usually mean the zodiac sign the Sun occupied
        when you were born. Astrologically, the Sun is associated with identity, vitality, creative
        purpose, and the qualities through which a person develops a stronger sense of self. I would
        not reduce that to a fixed personality description. Its house, aspects, rulerships,
        condition, and the person's own experience all matter.
      </p>

      <h2>The Moon: needs, instinct, and emotional memory</h2>
      <p>
        The Moon is associated with instinctive responses, emotional needs, memory, habits, and the
        ways we restore a sense of safety. These qualities can feel deeply familiar without being
        simple. A Moon sign changes expression through house, aspects, condition, and relationship
        to the rest of the chart.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={ocean.seaStackMoonrise} line="Three familiar pieces. One much larger pattern." />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>The rising sign: the eastern horizon</h2>
      <p>
        The rising sign, or Ascendant, is the zodiac sign rising over the eastern horizon at the
        moment and place of birth. Because Earth rotates, it changes throughout the day. The
        Ascendant also anchors the house framework, so two people born on the same day may share many
        planet-in-sign placements while having charts organized very differently.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p style="margin: 0;">
          <strong>This is why astrologers ask for your birth time.</strong> Without it, many planetary
          positions can still be used, but the Ascendant and house structure may be uncertain.
          <a href="/birth-time/">Here is how to track your birth time down</a> and how much precision can matter.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Even after the Big Three are clear, we are only beginning. The rest of the planets, houses,
        aspects, timing, and the life being lived all add context. Open the
        <a href="/tools/explore-your-chart/">interactive chart explorer</a> to practice combining the pieces.
      </p>
      <FurtherReading />
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Three pieces are only the beginning"
    body="Practice moving from labels to relationships: planet, sign, house, aspect, then the whole chart."
    buttonLabel="Open the chart explorer"
    buttonHref="/tools/explore-your-chart/"
    secondaryLabel="Learn birth chart basics"
    secondaryHref="/explore/birth-chart-basics/"
  />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .big-three-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-block: var(--space-8);
  }

  .big-three-card {
    padding: var(--space-6);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-color, currentColor);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg, 1rem);
  }

  .big-three-card h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
  }

  .big-three-card__glyph {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 7vw, 3.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
  }

  .synthesis {
    margin-block: var(--space-10);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .big-three-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
